{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>For the pious have vanished</b> — גמר is a participle. Or if it were from the root גמר, חסיד would be one who acts loyally; likewise פסו is cut off, like פס ידא (the hand has ceased)." "<b>And the faithful</b> — it is an adjective, and it is not thus שומר אמונים, for it is a noun."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The Hebrew alternates between treating גמר as verb and as noun; translated hyper-literally to preserve the compressed grammatical argument. The phrase גמר חסיד is read once as a verbal clause ('the pious have vanished') and once as a construct phrase ('completer of a pious one'), i.e., one who acts loyally.", 1 "Ibn Ezra argues that אמונים functions adjectivally here, not as a noun in construct."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:27:06.344452163Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}